  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "The equation ${x}^{2}+8x+9=0$ is equivalent to$\\,(x\\,\\,+$\n{{field-1}}\n$)^{2}=$\n{{field-2}}, and the solutions are \n\n$x=$\n{{field-3}}\n$\\pm $\n{{field-4}}.",
  "worked_solution": "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side and complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+8x+9 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+8x & =-9 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2\\cdot 4\\cdot x & =-9 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2\\cdot 4\\cdot x+{4}^{2}-{4}^{2} & =-9 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 4\\cdot x+{4}^{2})-16 & =-9 \\\\ (x+4)^{2}-16 & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+4)^{2}-16 & =-9 \\\\ (x+4)^{2} & =-9+16 \\\\ (x+4)^{2} & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve the equation using the square-root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+4)^{2} & =7 \\\\ \\sqrt{(x+4)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{7} \\\\ x+4 & =\\pm \\sqrt{7} \\\\ x & =-4\\pm \\sqrt{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
